## Account lockout

Wrong passwords are counted per account **and** client (its IP address):
`LOCKOUT_MAX_FAILURES` (5) in a row from one client lock sign-in to that
account *from that client* for `LOCKOUT_MINUTES` (15). Someone who knows your
email can only lock themselves out, never you. Guessing spread over many
addresses is stopped by a ceiling: `LOCKOUT_ACCOUNT_MAX_FAILURES` (50) from all
clients within `LOCKOUT_ACCOUNT_WINDOW_MINUTES` (60) lock the account itself.

While locked, every sign-in answers 423 `account_locked` with the minutes
left, whatever password was sent, so a right guess is never confirmed. A
successful sign-in clears that client's count (not the account-wide one); a
password reset clears everything. IP addresses are stored only as hashes.
